hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1622","**\nNarrated to us Ahmad ibn ‘Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi, who said: Narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam; and narrated to us Nafi‘ ibn Yazid, who said: Narrated to me Yahya ibn Abi Sulayman, from Yazid ibn Abi al-‘Itab and Ibn al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah (RA), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:\n\n\If you come to us while we are in prostration"","" then prostrate"","" and do not count it as anything. And whoever catches a rak‘ah has indeed caught the prayer.\""\n\nAbu Bakr (ibn Abi Khaythamah) said: Regarding the reliability of this chain"","" I was not certain about the integrity or criticism of Yahya ibn Abi Sulayman.\n\nAbu Bakr said: I then looked into it and found that Abu Sa‘id"","" the freed slave of Banu Hashim"","" had narrated from this Yahya ibn Abi Sulayman several reports.\n\nAbu Bakr said: This wording"","" \""do not count it as anything"",""\"" is of the type that I have explained in various places in our books"","" namely that the Arabs negate a name from something due to its deficiency and incompleteness. If the report is authentic from the Prophet (ﷺ)"","" then by saying"","" \""do not count it as anything"",""\"" he meant: Do not count it as a prostration that suffices for an obligatory rak‘ah of the prayer. He did not mean: Do not count it as anything at all"","" neither obligatory nor supererogatory.\n\n---"""""
